Separated product and service purchasing creates misaligned incentives between manufacturers and users. When manufacturers bear responsibility for product performance through service bundling, they gain economic motivation to design for durability and repairability. This internalizes lifecycle costs into production decisions.

Bundled models reduce consumer friction around maintenance decisions by establishing automatic care routines. Users gain payment certainty and simplified administration, while manufacturers build predictable revenue streams and direct customer relationships. Performance-based service contracts create feedback loops where manufacturer-collected product performance data directly informs next-generation design improvements.